    useThisWhen: "You have cold Bone Broth Rice in the fridge (this is mandatory — fresh rice = mushy mess) and you want 12 minutes of stovetop work for a dinner that feels like takeout. Eat it solo at 333 cal / 16g protein, or stack a protein on top for a 500-cal dinner with 35-40g protein.",
    flavorTarget: "Layered umami — soy gives salt + depth, sambal gives fermented heat, sesame chili oil gives aroma + nuttiness, Jet Tila's Umami Punch gives the MSG-style amplification that makes takeout fried rice hit. Rice should look lightly toasted and amber-coated, NOT drenched or wet.",
    splitNote: {
      adult: "Full sauce, full sambal. Optional: scatter sliced Thai chili and chili oil on top.",
      kid: "Most kids skip fried rice entirely — give them half a serving of plain Bone Broth Rice (microwave 60 sec) + their protein + raw mini sweet peppers instead. The fried rice complexity isn't worth the fight. If they DO want fried rice, halve the sambal and serve in a small bowl flipped onto the plate as a mound (kids respond to playful shapes).",
    },
    ingredients: [
      "--- TIER 3 BASE (REQUIRED COLD) ---",
      { text: "3 servings cold Bone Broth Rice (~2¼ cups cooked, made ahead and refrigerated)", link: "/cookbook/bone-broth-rice" },
      "--- TIER 2 PROTEIN ---",
      "2 large eggs",
      "--- TIER 4 FRESHNESS ---",
      "2 Thai chilies, finely sliced (mixed into the egg whisk for chili-flecked scramble)",
      "2 green onions, chopped",
      "Fresh chives, chopped (~2 tbsp)",
      "--- COOKING FAT ---",
      "2 tsp avocado oil (or any neutral high-smoke-point oil)",
      "--- INLINE SAUCE (makes 2 servings sauce, used in this 3-serving recipe — see Smart Upgrades for honest macros note) ---",
      "2 tbsp Kikkoman All-Purpose Soy Sauce",
      "2 tsp Huy Fong Sambal Oelek",
      "2 tsp Dynasty Sesame Chili Oil",
      "2 tsp Jet Tila's Umami Punch (Spiceology)",
    ],
    steps: [
      { text: "MAKE THE SAUCE: Stir together soy + sambal + sesame chili oil + Umami Punch in a small bowl or ramekin. Set aside. This is the entire 'sauce step' — no cooking, no reduction.", image: "/images/spicy-umami-fried-rice/sauce-mise-en-place.webp" },
      { text: "PREP MISE-EN-PLACE: Cold rice in a container, whisk eggs with the sliced Thai chilies, chop chives + green onion. Everything within arm's reach — the actual frying is fast.", image: "/images/spicy-umami-fried-rice/prep.webp" },
      { text: "EGGS, FIRST: Heat a wok or large skillet over medium-high. Add 1 tsp oil. Pour in the chili-flecked egg whisk and let it spread into a thin pancake.", image: "/images/spicy-umami-fried-rice/step1-egg-pour.webp" },
      { text: "SOFT SCRAMBLE: As the bottom sets (~30 seconds), break it up with the spatula into soft curds. Pull off heat at 80% set — they'll finish from carryover. Slide eggs to the side of the wok OR remove to a plate.", image: "/images/spicy-umami-fried-rice/step2-eggs-scrambled.webp" },
      { text: "TOAST THE RICE: Add the remaining 1 tsp oil to the hot wok. Add cold rice. Break up clumps with the spatula but DO NOT stir constantly — let the rice sit for 30-second intervals to develop slight toast on the bottom. Stir, sit, stir, sit. This is where the takeout texture lives.", image: "/images/spicy-umami-fried-rice/step3-rice-toast.webp" },
      { text: "POUR THE SAUCE: Pour the sauce around the edge of the wok (so it hits hot metal and sizzles, not directly on the rice). Toss continuously now to coat. Rice should turn light amber, not wet brown. If you see standing liquid, you used too much sauce — keep tossing until it cooks off.", image: "/images/spicy-umami-fried-rice/step4-sauce-pour.webp" },
      { text: "FINISH: Return scrambled eggs. Add green onions. Toss 30-60 seconds. Off heat.", image: "/images/spicy-umami-fried-rice/step5-coated.webp" },
      { text: "GARNISH AND SERVE: Scatter chopped chives across the top — the green hit matters visually AND lifts the dish. Plate in bowls. Adults: optional extra sliced Thai chili or chili oil drizzle on top. Kids: rice mound in a small bowl, inverted onto the plate. Done.", image: "/images/spicy-umami-fried-rice/hero.webp" },
    ],
    troubleshooting: [
      { problem: "Rice is mushy / clumpy", fix: "Used fresh-cooked rice. This recipe REQUIRES cold rice. Make Bone Broth Rice ahead and refrigerate overnight (minimum 4 hours / freezer 30 min in a pinch)." },
      { problem: "Wet brown rice instead of toasty amber", fix: "Oversauced OR didn't let the sauce cook off. Pour the sauce around the edge of the wok (not center), toss continuously, and keep going until standing liquid is gone." },
      { problem: "Flat / bland", fix: "Skimped on the sambal or the Umami Punch. These are the two flavor amplifiers — go full measure. The soy alone won't carry the dish." },
      { problem: "Too salty", fix: "Bone broth rice already has salt from the broth. Use exactly 2 tbsp soy (not 3), and skip any additional salt." },
      { problem: "Sticky / gummy bottom", fix: "Wok wasn't hot enough OR you stirred constantly. Get the wok medium-high BEFORE the rice goes in, and let the rice toast in 30-second intervals between stirs." },
      { problem: "Eggs went rubbery", fix: "Pulled at 100% set instead of 80%. Carryover heat in a hot wok finishes them — soft curds while they hit the rice, not dry chunks before." },
    ],
    smartUpgrades: [
      "Make it a real protein dinner: Add 6 oz cooked chicken thigh, shrimp, or steak per serving to push it to 500 cal / 38-42g protein. Honest framing: this base recipe is carb-dominant (38g carbs / 16g protein), and adding a protein is the canonical move. Crispy Chicken add-on is coming as its own recipe.",
      "Make it vegetarian: drop the eggs and add 1 cup frozen edamame + 2 tbsp roasted peanuts. Same flavor architecture, 12g plant protein.",
      "Make it lower-sodium: cut the soy to 1 tbsp + add 1 tsp rice vinegar. Same depth, half the salt.",
    ],
    brands: [
      { name: "Kikkoman", item: "All-Purpose Soy Sauce", why: "Tier 1 sauce backbone. Clean salt + umami — no preservatives, no surprises. The default soy for the site for a reason.", image: "/images/brands/kikkoman-soy-sauce.jpg", url: "https://kikkomanusa.com/homecooks/products/soy-sauce-non-gmo/" },
      { name: "Huy Fong Foods", item: "Sambal Oelek Ground Fresh Chili Paste", why: "Fermented chili heat with no sugar, no vinegar dominance — pure chili. The grown-up sriracha. 2 tsp here delivers heat + texture without making the rice wet.", image: "/images/brands/huy-fong-sambal-oelek.png", url: "https://www.huyfong.com/sambal-oelek/" },
      { name: "Dynasty", item: "Sesame Chili Oil (3.5 oz)", why: "Toasted sesame aroma + slow chili burn. The aromatic layer that makes the rice smell like a restaurant kitchen. 2 tsp is enough — this is calorie-dense oil, not a free-pour.", url: "https://www.amazon.com/Dynasty-Sesame-Chili-Oil-3-5/dp/B0001LJAW2" },
      { name: "Jet Tila's", item: "Umami Punch Ssamjang-Inspired Seasoning (Spiceology)", why: "The MSG-style amplifier. Doenjang + gochujang + sesame + garlic + green onion in a shaker. 2 tsp delivers the takeout-style 'how do they make it taste like THAT' depth at home.", image: "/images/brands/jet-tila-umami-punch.jpg", url: "https://spiceology.com/products/jet-tila-umami-punch-ssamjang-seasoning" },
      { name: "Bone Broth Rice", item: "Cookbook cross-link — the base", why: "This is THE base. Cold leftover bone broth rice is what makes the fried rice work. Make 8 servings on Sunday, fry on demand the rest of the week.", url: "/cookbook/bone-broth-rice" },
    ],
    macroHonesty: "Per-serving macros (333 cal / 16g protein / 12.5g fat / 35.5g carbs) are computed from the recipe ingredients. The Macrofactor app's 'Fried Rice Sauce (Nested)' entry shows 96 cal / 9g protein per sauce-serving, which materially overstates protein (soy + sambal + sesame oil + spice blend together can't deliver 9g protein in 2 tbsp of liquid). Site uses the ingredient-derived macros and flags them estimated. This is also a carb-dominant recipe by design — add a protein on top to push it into 'high-protein dinner' territory.",
